**What the research lane should take from this.** Slot-level Pearson correlations on 5-minute returns are owned by a handful of common-jump days. Any claim or denial of localized lead-lag needs Spearman, leave-top-5%-out, the reverse-direction correlation, and own-autocorrelation printed side by side. Cycle20's substantive conclusion stands: no receivable cross-market lead exists at this granularity. The FOMC-day continuation is an out-of-sample-only, 44-day, post-hoc cell that does not replicate on in-sample FOMC days, so it is not a promotion candidate.

**Counter-evidence to my own verdict, recorded in the entry.** The FOMC date list is from memory and unverified, though the calendar-free proxy (statement-bar |z|≥3) gives the same direction. The in-sample replication condition in my rule was weak enough that a tail-driven number passed it. The stale-rate tertile rule degenerated and was replaced by a binary split after the fact.

**Files written, all under AUREL_RESEARCH.** Two scripts, two result files, two run logs, and the findings entry. PROMOTED, backlog, and DATA_MAP are unchanged. No orders, no production edits, no process actions, no MT5 connection, no external requests, no scan. The task-observer log sits outside the write boundary and was not written, matching prior refute runs.
